[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Alerts and subscriptions (/docs/pr-preview/pr-39/platform/administration/alerts)\n\n\n\nSubscriptions turn Lamassu events into actionable notifications. You can alert a team, feed an incident system or trigger an automation when a CA, certificate or device changes.\n\nHow it works [#how-it-works]\n\n1. A service publishes a domain event.\n2. Lamassu evaluates the subscriptions associated with that event type.\n3. Each subscription's filter decides whether it should be notified.\n4. Lamassu delivers the event to the configured channel.\n\nThe event inventory shows when each type was last observed, how many times it has occurred and how many subscriptions it has. You can expand an event to inspect an example of its JSON payload before creating the filter.\n\nAvailable channels [#available-channels]\n\n* **Email** sends alerts to a person or an operational list. It only requires the destination address. Event content is rendered as HTML-escaped text, never as markup.\n* **Microsoft Teams** posts the notification to a channel through its webhook URL.\n* **Webhook** delivers the event to an automation, SIEM or incident system via `POST` or `PUT`.\n\nThe Microsoft Teams and Webhook channels accept an optional `http_client` block that authenticates the call to the receiver. `auth_mode` selects `noauth` (the default), `apikey`, `jwt` or `mtls`; each mode requires its own options:\n\n* `apikey_options` requires `header` and `key`.\n* `jwt_options` requires `oidc_client_id`, `oidc_client_secret` and an absolute `http` or `https` `oidc_well_known`.\n* `mtls_options` requires the inline PEM `cert_content` and `key_content`; values that reference files on the server are rejected.\n\nThe [alerts API reference](/docs/api-reference/alerts) documents the full `http_client` schema.\n\nCreate a subscription [#create-a-subscription]\n\n<Steps>\n  <Step>\n    Choose the event [#choose-the-event]\n\n    Open **Alerts**, locate the event type and select **Subscribe**. Review the latest JSON example to identify the fields you will need to filter on.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Configure the destination [#configure-the-destination]\n\n    Select Email, Microsoft Teams or Webhook and enter the channel details.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Limit the notifications [#limit-the-notifications]\n\n    Add a filter if you don't want to receive every instance of the event.\n\n    * **None** notifies all instances of the event.\n    * **JSON Path** evaluates one or more specific fields.\n    * **JSON Schema** accepts only payloads with a particular structure.\n    * **JavaScript** lets you express custom logic. Each filter runs for at most 100 milliseconds; a filter that exceeds this limit fails the condition and the notification is skipped with the error `javascript filter execution timed out`.\n\n    For example, `function (event) { return event.data.status == \"NO_IDENTITY\"; }` limits a subscription to devices without an identity.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Review and activate [#review-and-activate]\n\n    Check the event, channel and filter in the summary. Select **Confirm Subscription** to start delivery.\n  </Step>\n</Steps>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Configure the webhook HTTP client [#configure-the-webhook-http-client]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The Webhook and Microsoft Teams channels show this configuration in the subscribe form, at the channel step, and again when editing a subscription; Email does not use it. The fields map to the optional `http_client` block described above:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * **Log Level** selects `debug`, `info`, `warn` or `error`; `info` is the default.\n  * **Call Timeout** defaults to 10 seconds. The console rejects a value above 30 seconds with `Webhook timeout must be at most 30s.`; the API clamps the same value to the cap instead (see [Calls are bounded](#webhook-delivery-rules)).\n  * There is no TLS-verification switch for these channels, because the service always verifies the receiver certificate (see [TLS is always verified](#webhook-delivery-rules)).\n  * **HTTP Authentication** selects `No Authentication` (the default), `API Key` (API key value and Header Name, `X-API-Key` by default), `OIDC Client Credentials` (Client ID, Client Secret and Well-Known URL) or `Mutual TLS` (Client Certificate and Client Private Key pasted as PEM). A stored private key stays hidden until you select **Show**.\n  * A `http://` URL shows the console warning `Unencrypted HTTP connection` (see the [Credentials over http://](#webhook-delivery-rules) callout below).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The form validates inline against the same delivery rules: the webhook URL and the OIDC Well-Known URL must be absolute `http` or `https` URLs, an API-key header may not use a reserved name, and the mTLS certificate and key must be PEM content, not file paths.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The summary in the Confirm step shows the authentication as `None`, `OIDC client credentials`, `API key` or `Mutual TLS`. The subscription details show the authentication, the API key header name (never the value), the OIDC issuer URL, mTLS as `Configured` and a fixed `TLS verification: Verified` row. The API key, client secret and private key are not shown in the subscription details.\n</div>\n\nWebhook delivery rules [#webhook-delivery-rules]\n\nNotifications delivered through the Microsoft Teams and Webhook channels follow a fixed HTTP policy:\n\n* **TLS is always verified.** The receiver certificate is checked against the system trust store. `insecure_skip_verify` is enforced as `false` — including when it is set to `true` — so self-signed or otherwise untrusted receivers fail delivery.\n* **Redirects are not followed.** A `3xx` response fails with `unexpected status code: <n>: redirects are not followed`. Configure `webhook_url` with the final absolute `http` or `https` URL: an `http://` address that an ingress redirects to `https://` stops working.\n* **Calls are bounded.** A call defaults to a 10 second timeout; a configured `call_timeout` is capped at 30 seconds. Values above the cap are clamped, not rejected.\n* **The response body is ignored.** Delivery checks the status code only, so the receiver may answer with any body.\n* **API-key headers are restricted.** A custom `apikey_options.header` may not use names that the platform or receiving services trust — for example `X-Principal-Id`, `X-Request-Id`, `Host`, `Content-Type` or `Forwarded` — and no name may start with `X-Forwarded-`, `X-Envoy-` or `X-Lms-`. Comparisons are case-insensitive.\n* **Invalid configurations are rejected at subscribe time.** A channel configuration that would fail on every delivery returns HTTP 400 with a body that starts with `struct validation error:`, and the subscription is not created.\n\n<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"Credentials over http://\">\n  Credentials are sent regardless of the URL scheme. With an `http://` `webhook_url`, the API key or bearer token travels in cleartext and mTLS has no effect. Prefer `https://` for `webhook_url`, `oidc_well_known` and the token endpoint it advertises.\n</Callout>\n\nManage a subscription [#manage-a-subscription]\n\nOpen an existing subscription to review its destination and filter, modify the configuration or select **Unsubscribe**. Remove subscriptions that no longer have an operational owner to avoid ignored deliveries or stale endpoints.\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Subscriptions created with older console versions may store the webhook target under `url` and `method` instead of `webhook_url` and `webhook_method`. The service delivers only through `webhook_url` and `webhook_method`, so such a subscription does not deliver even though the subscription details show the stored target. Open the subscription and save it again: the edit form prefills the stored target, and saving rewrites the channel configuration in the current layout.\n</div>\n\nEvent families [#event-families]\n\n* **Authorities:** `ca.create`, `ca.import`, `ca.reissue` and `ca.delete`.\n* **Certificates:** `ca.sign.certificate` and `certificate.delete`.\n* **Profiles:** `profile.issuance.create`, `profile.issuance.update` and `profile.issuance.delete`.\n* **Enrollment:** `dms.create`, `dms.update`, `dms.enroll` and `dms.reenroll`.\n* **Devices:** `device.create`, `device.identity.update` and `device.status.update`.\n* **Validation:** `va.role.crl.create`.\n* **Keys:** `kms.create`, `kms.import`, `kms.sign` and `kms.delete`.\n\nUse the catalog shown in the console as the source for the types available in your deployed version.\n"]
